WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.150 --> 00:00:09.360
Hello and welcome back to another
episode with ItsDrDan, and today we're

00:00:09.360 --> 00:00:14.070
going to be looking at phase changes,
specifically how to calculate and

00:00:14.070 --> 00:00:19.130
use the heat of vaporization and the
heat of fusion to calculate heat.

00:00:19.450 --> 00:00:23.940
So whenever we have a phase change,
what, what are we talking about?

00:00:24.460 --> 00:00:28.229
So let's say if I have a solid ice
cube here in the middle, and it's

00:00:28.229 --> 00:00:32.089
made of all these little waters,
all oriented in a very crystalline

00:00:32.090 --> 00:00:35.790
structure, you can see all the little
solids, all really close together.

00:00:35.820 --> 00:00:40.970
Now, as they start absorbing heat,
And start gaining, um, energy

00:00:40.970 --> 00:00:44.089
as a result, it will start to
move a little bit farther apart.

00:00:44.470 --> 00:00:48.359
The intermolecular forces will
start to drift farther and

00:00:48.360 --> 00:00:50.709
farther apart as they gain energy.

00:00:51.239 --> 00:00:54.359
And it's going to start
making a liquid as a result.

00:00:54.930 --> 00:00:59.069
Now when that happens, right, going
from a solid to a liquid, right, that is

00:00:59.069 --> 00:01:01.309
when we're referring to melting, right?

00:01:01.570 --> 00:01:03.690
And the opposite is all about freezing.

00:01:04.310 --> 00:01:07.670
So when we first melt, right, those
intermolecular forces are really far

00:01:07.670 --> 00:01:10.490
apart, it goes through a phase change.

00:01:11.070 --> 00:01:14.479
That is when we have to use
the heat of fusion to calculate

00:01:14.509 --> 00:01:15.770
that, the numbers behind that.

00:01:16.409 --> 00:01:19.890
Now, if that liquid is sitting there
for a long time and it keeps absorbing

00:01:19.920 --> 00:01:24.220
energy, eventually what will happen is
a lot of those little water molecules,

00:01:24.260 --> 00:01:28.480
they'll eventually break into the gas
phase, making a big gas cloud where all

00:01:28.480 --> 00:01:30.480
of them are little, are vapor particles.

00:01:30.875 --> 00:01:34.585
Um, that can eventually ricochet and
bounce off the different walls of

00:01:34.585 --> 00:01:36.035
the container that they might be in.

00:01:36.545 --> 00:01:39.705
And we would have to use the
heat of vaporization to talk

00:01:39.715 --> 00:01:41.134
about that phase change.

00:01:41.574 --> 00:01:46.985
Which, whenever we are either going
through boiling or vaporization.

00:01:47.515 --> 00:01:50.525
That is when it goes
from a liquid to a gas.

00:01:50.985 --> 00:01:54.585
So, we are going to be looking at
that and trying to understand that

00:01:54.585 --> 00:01:56.124
complete picture in this video.

00:01:56.755 --> 00:02:01.184
So, this is for introductory chem,
um, and, and slightly for gen chem

00:02:01.195 --> 00:02:02.795
one for a lot of students out there.

00:02:03.054 --> 00:02:07.664
But it's just the basics on how to
use delta H and use that with moles

00:02:07.664 --> 00:02:09.164
to go through different calculations.

00:02:09.505 --> 00:02:12.005
So let's take a look, and if this is
something that's really interesting

00:02:12.005 --> 00:02:13.515
to you, please hit that like button.

00:02:14.114 --> 00:02:17.424
Sub to the channel and you can see
more videos on topics like these.

00:02:17.715 --> 00:02:18.905
All right, let's go into it.

00:02:28.305 --> 00:02:31.584
All right, let's start with the
fundamentals, start with the basics.

00:02:31.914 --> 00:02:36.235
So let's first review of what I just
said before, which is if you are

00:02:36.235 --> 00:02:41.295
having a change of state or or a phase
change, both these words are generally

00:02:41.295 --> 00:02:45.815
used interchangeably depending on
who your professor is and who and

00:02:45.815 --> 00:02:47.284
where you are learning chemistry.

00:02:47.740 --> 00:02:51.700
So, whenever we talk about a phase,
we have a solid, liquid, or a gas.

00:02:51.720 --> 00:02:53.010
Same idea as states.

00:02:53.260 --> 00:02:54.760
We have a solid, liquid, or a gas.

00:02:55.220 --> 00:02:59.710
So if I'm going from a solid to a
liquid, that is whenever if I take an

00:02:59.730 --> 00:03:03.779
ice cube and you were to have it in your
hand, what is going to happen to it?

00:03:04.099 --> 00:03:07.390
Well, that ice cube is going
to eventually melt, right?

00:03:07.940 --> 00:03:11.570
Now if you take your melted water and
stick it back in the freezer, well

00:03:11.570 --> 00:03:13.570
that liquid, if you go backwards.

00:03:14.040 --> 00:03:15.119
Is going to freeze.

00:03:15.879 --> 00:03:20.319
Now, when we have these phase
changes, this is where the heat of

00:03:20.329 --> 00:03:25.059
fusion is going to be used, okay,
and we'll go over what formula.

00:03:25.460 --> 00:03:27.720
that will be required during that point.

00:03:28.630 --> 00:03:33.820
Now when you have the other phase change,
let's say if I go from a liquid to a

00:03:33.820 --> 00:03:37.870
gas, meaning that these liquid particles,
these intermolecular forces are going

00:03:37.870 --> 00:03:41.859
to drift so far apart that eventually
we're able to break the intermolecular

00:03:41.870 --> 00:03:44.360
forces, taking a lot of energy to do that.

00:03:44.920 --> 00:03:49.510
This is whenever we have, uh, it's either
it's going to be considered to be boiling

00:03:50.220 --> 00:03:54.170
or vaporization to go into the gas phase.

00:03:55.275 --> 00:03:58.845
Now, if you were to take those gases,
you were to cool them back down, right?

00:03:58.845 --> 00:04:00.145
They have a lot of kinetic energy.

00:04:00.145 --> 00:04:03.585
They're moving around very, because
you put so much heat into them.

00:04:04.125 --> 00:04:08.175
If you were to cool them back
down, well, that is now going to

00:04:08.175 --> 00:04:10.624
be referred to as condensation.

00:04:11.074 --> 00:04:12.325
It's the cooling, right?

00:04:12.345 --> 00:04:13.484
Bringing it down.

00:04:14.335 --> 00:04:16.205
So we, we go through condensation.

00:04:16.205 --> 00:04:19.245
If you think of like, uh, if you wake
up really early in the morning when

00:04:19.245 --> 00:04:23.784
it's cold outside, you'll see dew on
the grass, on everything that's outside.

00:04:24.075 --> 00:04:25.715
This is condensation.

00:04:26.250 --> 00:04:31.660
Now when that happens, this is going to be
referred to as the heat of vaporization.

00:04:32.815 --> 00:04:35.145
to how to calculate
these different values.

00:04:35.985 --> 00:04:41.535
Now when it comes to heat, so usually what
we'll use is the letter Q to denote heat.

00:04:42.225 --> 00:04:45.844
Now when it comes to anything
that's energetic related, we have

00:04:45.844 --> 00:04:50.075
to realize that there are signs
behind these because there's

00:04:50.084 --> 00:04:53.355
endothermic and exothermic processes.

00:04:54.315 --> 00:04:57.455
So with this, if you were to
have an ice cube in your hand and

00:04:57.455 --> 00:05:02.370
you were to hold it there, Is it
absorbing heat or releasing heat?

00:05:02.650 --> 00:05:03.900
And think about it for a second.

00:05:03.980 --> 00:05:06.780
Is what's happening to that
ice cube, is it getting hotter?

00:05:07.219 --> 00:05:08.750
Well, that means it's yes, right?

00:05:08.760 --> 00:05:10.219
It's absorbing heat.

00:05:10.520 --> 00:05:12.500
So absorbing is a positive value.

00:05:13.020 --> 00:05:16.350
So if you're melting, you're
going to have a positive value.

00:05:16.680 --> 00:05:19.860
And if you're boiling, you're
going to have a positive Q value.

00:05:20.735 --> 00:05:26.015
Versus, let's say if you want to
freeze it or to condense it, the

00:05:26.085 --> 00:05:30.154
ice or the water, whatever phase it
is, needs to lose heat, it needs to

00:05:30.154 --> 00:05:32.135
release it in order to get colder.

00:05:32.505 --> 00:05:38.324
So whenever we are going into the opposite
process of condensation and freezing, that

00:05:38.324 --> 00:05:41.384
is going to be a negative Q value, okay?

00:05:41.824 --> 00:05:45.365
So it all depends on whether
or not you are going one way or

00:05:45.365 --> 00:05:48.455
the other will reflect what you
will see in your calculation.

00:05:49.235 --> 00:05:52.195
Let's take a look at the different
formulas you will be using.

00:05:52.195 --> 00:05:59.915
So these are both of them here, so that
for Q, both of these are, depends on

00:05:59.924 --> 00:06:04.245
delta H fusion, delta H vaporization,
usually they have a little subscript

00:06:04.254 --> 00:06:09.434
that tells you which one you're going
to use, so Q is our heat value when

00:06:09.434 --> 00:06:16.440
we are looking at this, N is always
referred to as moles, and And delta H is

00:06:16.440 --> 00:06:22.270
something that we call as enthalpy, but
for most intensive purposes it's just

00:06:22.270 --> 00:06:26.530
delta H effusion just to keep it a little
easier for most introductory students.

00:06:27.050 --> 00:06:31.290
So we're multiplying these two concepts
together in order to calculate our heat.

00:06:31.799 --> 00:06:35.020
Let's take a look at some different
examples of the kinds of problems

00:06:35.029 --> 00:06:38.270
that you will see and how to solve
for this portion, how to plug in

00:06:38.270 --> 00:06:39.230
our different values for this.

00:06:39.280 --> 00:06:42.889
Our first example, calculate
the heat when 36 grams of

00:06:42.900 --> 00:06:45.669
water is melted at 0 degrees C.

00:06:46.410 --> 00:06:48.850
Okay, so how exactly do we do this?

00:06:48.870 --> 00:06:54.990
Well, it's going to require us to use
our, our Q equation for a phase change.

00:06:55.340 --> 00:06:59.689
So this is going to be N times
delta H, and being that it's

00:06:59.689 --> 00:07:05.429
referring to fusion, usually it's
abbreviated as F U S, or fusion,

00:07:05.459 --> 00:07:07.089
when we are seeing these equations.

00:07:07.630 --> 00:07:10.950
So the first thing you need to do
is we have, what were we given?

00:07:10.950 --> 00:07:13.000
We need to stay organized
with these things.

00:07:13.465 --> 00:07:16.545
We were given a mass value, which is 36.

00:07:16.855 --> 00:07:19.525
0 grams, that is not a mole value.

00:07:20.115 --> 00:07:23.245
And we were given a delta H of fusion.

00:07:23.865 --> 00:07:26.275
So what exactly do we have to do first?

00:07:26.275 --> 00:07:29.124
Well, if you can think of all of
our different calculations when

00:07:29.124 --> 00:07:33.635
it comes to masses and moles,
our goal is always the mole.

00:07:34.205 --> 00:07:37.585
So the first thing that we need
to do is convert grams to moles.

00:07:38.040 --> 00:07:41.280
So, one thing that I did here
is include the values for how

00:07:41.280 --> 00:07:42.620
to get to molar mass, right?

00:07:42.630 --> 00:07:46.120
And if you need help with molar
mass, refer to my videos on molar

00:07:46.120 --> 00:07:48.180
mass and using mole equations.

00:07:49.050 --> 00:07:53.230
So, for this one, right, we have
water is what's being used, so we

00:07:53.230 --> 00:07:58.950
need to add up one oxygen atom and
two hydrogen atoms to convert this.

00:07:59.840 --> 00:08:04.000
So, when we are doing this little
calculation here for ourselves, and

00:08:04.000 --> 00:08:05.879
here let's make some room for ourselves.

00:08:06.824 --> 00:08:12.114
This is going to be 18 grams for water,
and this is just rounded just to make

00:08:12.114 --> 00:08:13.884
it a little easier math on ourselves.

00:08:14.604 --> 00:08:17.244
And that's equivalent to
every 1 mole of water.

00:08:17.854 --> 00:08:22.544
So that's going to be
equal to 2 moles of H2O.

00:08:24.835 --> 00:08:27.604
We have our mole value, and what we're
going to do is we're going to plug

00:08:27.604 --> 00:08:32.865
both the delta H of fusion and the mole
value into this equation up here, right?

00:08:32.865 --> 00:08:34.575
So we're going to plug both parts in.

00:08:35.204 --> 00:08:37.345
So 2 moles is going to go in for N.

00:08:37.829 --> 00:08:38.970
And then the 6.

00:08:39.030 --> 00:08:41.900
02 kilojoules is going to
go in for delta fusion.

00:08:42.659 --> 00:08:44.049
So let's solve.

00:08:44.459 --> 00:08:46.569
If we have Q that's going to be times 2.

00:08:46.600 --> 00:08:49.990
00 moles times 6.

00:08:50.050 --> 00:08:52.860
02 kilojoules per mole.

00:08:53.800 --> 00:08:58.020
And what we are going to quickly see
is that moles cancels moles and what

00:08:58.020 --> 00:09:01.390
we will be left with is, um, 12.

00:09:01.469 --> 00:09:07.839
04 kilojoules for our answer, and we
will round that to the same number as

00:09:07.849 --> 00:09:13.359
sig figs that we had in our, in our
original problem, so this would be 12.

00:09:13.800 --> 00:09:14.759
0 kilojoules.

00:09:15.240 --> 00:09:17.590
Now, is this positive or is it negative?

00:09:18.039 --> 00:09:22.319
Well, being that it was melting, it
needed to absorb heat, so this would

00:09:22.319 --> 00:09:24.959
stay as a positive value, so 12.

00:09:25.000 --> 00:09:25.329
0.

00:09:26.030 --> 00:09:28.970
Now the follow up question is,
how much heat is required to

00:09:29.060 --> 00:09:31.149
freeze the exact same sample?

00:09:31.499 --> 00:09:33.910
So instead of melting it,
how would you freeze it?

00:09:34.489 --> 00:09:35.439
Well, think about it.

00:09:36.020 --> 00:09:38.630
One way is, it's just all
about the sign changes.

00:09:38.969 --> 00:09:42.439
So before, it was a positive 12.

00:09:42.669 --> 00:09:46.170
0 kilojoules to melt it, right?

00:09:46.949 --> 00:09:48.999
So what would it be in order to freeze it?

00:09:49.020 --> 00:09:50.420
Well, it's going to be the opposite.

00:09:50.459 --> 00:09:52.339
So it'd be negative 12.

00:09:52.400 --> 00:09:55.860
0 kilojoules in order to freeze it.

00:09:56.230 --> 00:10:00.020
It's all about how that
delta H's sign changes.

00:10:00.490 --> 00:10:05.449
So when it comes to delta H of
fusion for freezing, instead

00:10:05.460 --> 00:10:06.840
that would have been a 6.

00:10:06.910 --> 00:10:10.069
02, and it's negative
for kilojoules per mole.

00:10:10.669 --> 00:10:15.239
Alright, so the sign of our
delta H changes depending on

00:10:15.239 --> 00:10:16.930
if it's positive or negative.

00:10:17.255 --> 00:10:19.605
Whether we're freezing it or melting it.

00:10:21.145 --> 00:10:23.045
Let's go on to our next problem.

00:10:23.415 --> 00:10:28.224
To reduce swelling, a 237 gram
ice pack is placed on the body.

00:10:28.875 --> 00:10:33.165
How much heat in joules will it
take to melt the ice and warm up

00:10:33.175 --> 00:10:36.634
the liquid water to 37 degrees C?

00:10:37.325 --> 00:10:40.675
Okay, this is something that
combines different concepts.

00:10:41.145 --> 00:10:43.844
So there's another video that was
released at the same time as this

00:10:43.844 --> 00:10:45.474
one, which is all about specific heat.

00:10:45.930 --> 00:10:49.340
And there was a video released a few
months ago on how to do heat as well.

00:10:49.970 --> 00:10:53.690
So this is something that it
requires us to understand how do

00:10:53.700 --> 00:10:55.510
these energy diagrams look like.

00:10:56.200 --> 00:11:00.570
So at first, it talks about a phase,
it talks about a phase change, right?

00:11:00.570 --> 00:11:03.260
We have an ice pack, the ice is melting.

00:11:03.829 --> 00:11:08.859
So whenever we have these diagrams, we
got to refer to what the temperatures are.

00:11:09.269 --> 00:11:11.839
So in order for something to
freeze, it's at zero degrees C.

00:11:12.529 --> 00:11:17.990
So when, in order to convert that,
It is a straight line that you see.

00:11:18.390 --> 00:11:23.630
This is referring to the ice pack
melting because it takes so much

00:11:23.670 --> 00:11:28.389
energy for those intermolecular forces
to get far enough apart so that way

00:11:28.389 --> 00:11:29.839
it can actually become a liquid.

00:11:30.569 --> 00:11:36.640
Now afterwards what was happening
is it was heated up to 37 degrees.

00:11:37.200 --> 00:11:43.130
So usually what we'll see is,
is a slope referring to heating.

00:11:43.794 --> 00:11:46.855
Now, if the slope was going the opposite
direction, that would be cooling.

00:11:47.465 --> 00:11:48.785
Now, why do we care about this?

00:11:49.224 --> 00:11:53.274
This is because there are, there are
multiple heat equations that work here.

00:11:53.915 --> 00:12:00.014
We have what I refer to as Q1 and
Q2, being that we're going to be

00:12:00.015 --> 00:12:02.619
doing two separate equations here.

00:12:03.300 --> 00:12:05.230
So now, why do we care?

00:12:05.230 --> 00:12:06.730
It's because we want to stay organized.

00:12:07.480 --> 00:12:11.200
So Q1 is what we just did in
our previous problem, right?

00:12:11.560 --> 00:12:14.429
N times delta H fusion.

00:12:14.520 --> 00:12:18.729
Because it's all about how is this
ice cube melting in your ice pack.

00:12:19.610 --> 00:12:24.250
Q2 is all about the MCAT equation, right?

00:12:24.380 --> 00:12:27.260
Um, so it's MC delta T.

00:12:27.910 --> 00:12:30.520
Something else that's covered
in this type of chapter.

00:12:31.250 --> 00:12:34.580
And what we are trying to do is we're
trying to figure out what the total

00:12:34.580 --> 00:12:37.550
heat that is required to cool this down.

00:12:38.220 --> 00:12:43.570
So in order to do the very last
part, to find the Q total, we

00:12:43.570 --> 00:12:50.260
gotta add up Q one plus Q two
in order to get our final value.

00:12:50.989 --> 00:12:54.400
So we got to do a little bit of
work here, but let's first take

00:12:54.400 --> 00:12:56.699
a look at what we did last time.

00:12:57.140 --> 00:12:59.519
So the first thing we
need is our mass, right?

00:12:59.540 --> 00:13:05.470
It was 237 grams is what is
being given to us in the problem.

00:13:05.470 --> 00:13:08.270
237 grams of water to be exact.

00:13:08.959 --> 00:13:13.260
So when we go through that, we
need to convert that to moles in

00:13:13.329 --> 00:13:15.540
order to use our first equation.

00:13:16.120 --> 00:13:18.799
So 237 grams of water.

00:13:19.470 --> 00:13:22.569
In order to convert that to moles,
we have to once again use our

00:13:22.569 --> 00:13:29.260
molar mass, which is 18 grams of
water is equivalent to one mole.

00:13:29.745 --> 00:13:30.425
of water.

00:13:32.395 --> 00:13:38.285
Grams cancels grams, and what we
are going to get is once we do 237

00:13:38.615 --> 00:13:45.365
divided by 18, we are going to get
about, and after rounding, is 13.

00:13:45.755 --> 00:13:47.445
2 moles of water.

00:13:47.735 --> 00:13:52.935
Okay, now what we can do is with
that value is plug it in to our

00:13:52.935 --> 00:13:56.015
equation from earlier, so that 13.

00:13:56.045 --> 00:13:58.395
2 moles can get plugged in for n.

00:13:59.305 --> 00:14:04.135
And being that I, that we have the
delta H value as well, which was 6.

00:14:04.194 --> 00:14:07.285
02 times, uh, 6.

00:14:07.285 --> 00:14:10.305
02 kilojoules, that can get
plugged in for delta H effusion.

00:14:11.214 --> 00:14:12.584
So let's solve Q1.

00:14:13.854 --> 00:14:19.214
So Q1 is, after getting
all of our values, is 13.

00:14:19.215 --> 00:14:21.885
2 moles times 6.

00:14:21.955 --> 00:14:25.735
02 kilojoules for every mole.

00:14:27.095 --> 00:14:30.064
We can now see that the
moles will cancel moles.

00:14:30.394 --> 00:14:33.124
So that's another thing, you can
always let the units kind of guide

00:14:33.124 --> 00:14:34.854
your thinking and your process here.

00:14:35.755 --> 00:14:43.155
Um, and what we will have after
doing the math is about, um, 79.

00:14:43.355 --> 00:14:46.315
5 kilojoules for Q1.

00:14:48.655 --> 00:14:49.145
Q2.

00:14:49.145 --> 00:14:52.385
So Q2 is using the MCAT equation.

00:14:52.875 --> 00:14:55.235
So what exactly is happening here?

00:14:55.665 --> 00:14:56.845
Well, we have our mass, right?

00:14:56.845 --> 00:14:58.125
That's a 237.

00:14:58.604 --> 00:15:00.155
What about delta T?

00:15:00.235 --> 00:15:04.195
So delta T, for those of you
who might not realize, that is

00:15:04.195 --> 00:15:07.295
the TF, so final minus initial.

00:15:07.985 --> 00:15:10.215
Now what was our initial value?

00:15:10.215 --> 00:15:14.125
Well, looking at our curve, that
was about 0 degrees Celsius.

00:15:14.780 --> 00:15:17.970
And end it at about 37 degrees C.

00:15:18.550 --> 00:15:25.069
So this is our TF and our TI in our
original equations that we have here.

